{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "04", "section": "02", "chapter_title": "AI Conversations as a Teacher", "title": "Scenario-Based AI Dialogue", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting scenario-based ai dialogue to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher", "cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/04/01"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_calibration"]}, "content": "# Scenario-Based AI Dialogue\n\n![Scenario-Based AI Dialogue textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-02-scenario-based-ai-dialogue.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA single long prompt that asks for everything at once usually returns a vague answer, because the AI has to balance too many goals in one shot. A scenario-based dialogue solves this by breaking the work into a planned sequence of turns, where each turn does exactly one job: set the situation, narrow the constraints, then stress-test the result. You stop hoping for a perfect first reply and instead steer the conversation toward a usable one.\n\nThis matters because the value lives in the steering, and the steering has to be visible. When each turn is labeled with its job, a reviewer can read your transcript and see which turn caused which improvement. An unlabeled wall of chat hides your reasoning; a labeled sequence makes it auditable, which is what turns a chat log into evidence of skill.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student needs a 90-second welcome to read aloud to a new study-group member. They type one giant prompt: \"write a warm, short, professional, reassuring welcome for a nervous new student who missed orientation, no jargon, friendly but not childish.\" The reply is 300 words and uses the phrase \"academic onboarding.\" It satisfies every adjective a little and none of them well.\n\nThe same student, working in turns, gets a tighter result. Turn one sets the situation. Turn two adds the single hard limit (\"under 90 seconds aloud, no jargon\"). Turn three pressure-tests it (\"what if they already feel behind?\"). Each turn fixes one thing, and the transcript shows exactly where the length dropped and where the reassurance line appeared.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA scenario-based dialogue has three structural moves, run in order. Skipping or merging them is what produces vague output.\n\n| Move | Turn job | What it controls |\n|------|----------|------------------|\n| SET CONTEXT | name the situation and the audience | who the answer is for and what counts as success |\n| NARROW | add one hard constraint at a time | length, vocabulary, format, or tone |\n| STRESS-TEST | introduce a complication | robustness when the easy case breaks |\n\nThe reason for one job per turn is diagnosis. If you change three things in a single message and the reply gets worse, you cannot tell which change caused it. Isolating one move per turn means every improvement and every regression has a single visible cause. This is the same logic a programmer uses when changing one variable at a time to find a bug.\n\n<inline-check question=\"In one message you write: 'Make it under 90 seconds, drop the jargon, and add a reassurance line for a nervous student.' The reply gets worse. What is the structural problem?\" explanation=\"The message merges three jobs (a NARROW for length, a NARROW for vocabulary, and a STRESS-TEST for nerves) into one turn. Because three things changed at once, you cannot tell which one caused the regression. One job per turn is what gives every change a single visible cause.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Three jobs changed in one turn, so no single change has a visible cause\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The turn was too polite\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It needed more adjectives to be specific\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing; merging jobs always speeds up the dialogue\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nLabeling is not decoration. Writing the turn's job (\"Turn 2: NARROW\") next to the message converts a private chat into a readable record. A reviewer can replay your reasoning turn by turn and check that each move actually did its job, which is the standard this section holds you to.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA scenario-based dialogue is a concrete artifact: a saved chat transcript where each turn has a labeled job, so a reader can see how the conversation drove toward a usable answer. A freshman builds it turn by turn. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. Write the scenario the AI must answer inside, as one paragraph:\n\n```text\nSCENARIO: A new student joined my study group mid-semester and missed\nthe syllabus walkthrough. I am the peer leader. Draft what I say in our\nfirst 5-minute meeting.\n```\n\nStep 2. Plan three labeled turns before typing, so each turn does exactly one job:\n\n| Turn | Job | What you type |\n|------|-----|---------------|\n| 1 | SET CONTEXT | paste the scenario; name the audience (a nervous new student) |\n| 2 | NARROW | \"Keep it under 90 seconds to read aloud; no jargon\" |\n| 3 | STRESS-TEST | \"What if the student already feels behind? Add one reassurance line\" |\n\nStep 3. Run the turns and save the full transcript. Turn 2 cut the length; Turn 3 added a reassurance sentence. Because each turn is labeled, a reader can tell which turn caused which change. An unlabeled blob would hide that.\n\nStep 4. Log the accept/reject. Turn 3 returned two reassurance lines; you kept \"Everyone here joined at a different point\" and cut the second because it just repeated the first. The labeled transcript plus that one cut is the evidence: a reader can replay your reasoning turn by turn.\n\nPut the three moves in the order that gives each change a diagnosable cause:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"SET CONTEXT: paste the scenario and name the audience (a nervous new student)\", \"NARROW: add one hard limit (under 90 seconds aloud, no jargon)\", \"STRESS-TEST: introduce a complication (what if they already feel behind?)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nNow walk one turn at a time, predicting what each turn will and will not fix:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the three labeled turns\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"After only the SET CONTEXT turn, the reply is 300 words of generic welcome. What does the next turn need to do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"NARROW: add one hard constraint (under 90 seconds, no jargon)\",\"Add three more adjectives in one message\",\"Accept the 300-word reply as final\",\"Start a brand-new chat\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"NARROW. Turn 2 adds the single length-and-vocabulary limit, and the reply drops from 300 words to about 70. Because only one thing changed, you can attribute the drop to that turn.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The welcome is now short but assumes the student feels fine. Which turn tests robustness?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"STRESS-TEST: \\\"What if the student already feels behind? Add one reassurance line.\\\"\",\"Another NARROW shortening it further\",\"Re-paste the original scenario\",\"Ask for a background color\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"STRESS-TEST. Turn 3 introduces the complication, and the reply adds a reassurance sentence. The useful content appeared only after NARROW and STRESS-TEST, not from SET CONTEXT alone.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Save the full labeled transcript. A reviewer can now see Turn 2 caused the length cut and Turn 3 caused the reassurance line, which is what turns a chat log into evidence of steering.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nPlan the turns before you type the first one. A dialogue is well-built when every turn has a named job and the transcript shows a visible cause for each change in the output.\n\nCheck the transcript against three questions:\n\n1. **One-job check:** Does each turn change exactly one thing (context, a constraint, or a complication)?\n2. **Cause check:** Can you point to the turn that produced each improvement, rather than a general \"the conversation got better\"?\n3. **Stress check:** Did at least one turn introduce a complication, so the final answer is tested, not just polished for the easy case?\n\nIf two turns change several things at once, split them. You will not be able to defend which move mattered, and neither will a reviewer.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is thinking more turns automatically means better dialogue. Ten turns that each tweak wording are weaker than three turns that set context, narrow, and stress-test. Quality comes from distinct jobs, not from quantity.\n\nThe second misreading is saving only the final answer and discarding the path. The final answer alone proves nothing about your skill; anyone can copy a good paragraph. The labeled path is the artifact, because it shows you steered the result on purpose.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the three moves to a new scenario: convincing a hesitant classmate to join a group presentation. Turn one (SET CONTEXT) names the audience, a shy classmate who fears public speaking, and the goal, a two-line invitation. The first reply is generic encouragement. Turn two (NARROW) adds the constraint \"offer one specific low-pressure role,\" and the reply now suggests \"you could run the slides while we talk.\" Turn three (STRESS-TEST) asks \"what if they say they will freeze on stage?\" and the reply adds a fallback (they can read from notes).\n\nNotice that the useful content, the specific role and the fallback, only appeared after the NARROW and STRESS-TEST turns. The SET CONTEXT turn alone gave you nothing actionable. That is the lesson: the early turn frames the problem, but the later turns extract the parts you can actually use. Reading the transcript, anyone can see that progression and trust that you built the answer rather than copied it.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is \"this turn improved the output\"; the evidence is the labeled turn and the specific change it produced. A strong reader does not say \"the dialogue worked\"; they say \"Turn 2 cut the welcome from 300 words to 70 because it added the length limit.\"\n\nA strong reader of Scenario-Based AI Dialogue can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which turn carried the most weight in reaching a usable answer?\n2. Which turn, if removed, would leave the answer vague or untested?\n3. What complication did the stress-test turn introduce, and did the AI handle it?\n4. If you ran the same scenario again, which turn would you reorder or split?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when the labeled transcript lets a reviewer reconstruct your steering without your narration. Keep the full sequence, mark each turn's job, and add a one-line accept/reject note for at least one AI suggestion you did not take whole.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"Turn 3 returned two reassurance lines; I kept the one about everyone joining at a different point and cut the duplicate. The cut matters because the reader has 90 seconds, and a repeated reassurance wastes time that the labeled transcript shows I was protecting.\" That ties a concrete edit to the constraint you set earlier in the dialogue.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the dialogue from a low-stakes welcome to a high-stakes message: a note to a struggling teammate about missed deadlines. The three-move structure transfers, but the STRESS-TEST turn changes character. For a welcome, you stress-tested for nerves; here you stress-test for defensiveness (\"what if they feel blamed?\"). The NARROW turn also tightens, since tone now carries real consequences. The skeleton (context, narrow, stress-test) holds across both, but the complication you choose to test depends on what could go wrong in the new setting. Understanding the structure means knowing which turn to re-aim, not retyping the old one.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how scenario dialogue connects to the chapter's wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-02-scenario-based-ai-dialogue-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 04.02. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your transcript labels each turn's job, shows a visible cause for each change, includes one stress-test turn, and logs at least one accepted and one rejected suggestion.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue", "cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"], "stem": "In a three-turn scenario dialogue, which turn does the job of NARROW?", "options": ["The turn that pastes the scenario and names the audience.", "The turn that says 'Keep it under 90 seconds to read aloud; no jargon.'", "The turn that asks 'What if the student already feels behind?'", "The turn that copies the whole AI reply into the artifact."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "NARROW adds the constraint (length, tone). Pasting the scenario is SET CONTEXT; the 'what if' is STRESS-TEST.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_one_giant_prompt", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue"], "stem": "A scenario reads only: 'A new student joined late. Draft what I say.' What constraint is missing that the model needs?", "options": ["Nothing; the situation is enough.", "Audience, length, and tone (for example: nervous new student, under 90 seconds, no jargon).", "The student's full name and ID number.", "A request to make the answer longer."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A scenario without constraints lets the model guess. Audience, length, and tone make the output usable.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_no_scenario_constraints", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue"], "stem": "Turn 3 returns two reassurance lines that say nearly the same thing. What is the strongest move for the saved artifact?", "options": ["Keep both lines so the artifact is longer.", "Keep one line and log that you cut the duplicate and why.", "Delete the whole transcript and start over in one prompt.", "Ask the AI to add a third reassurance line."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Selecting one line and logging the cut makes your judgment part of the evidence; keeping both adds no information.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_keep_whole_output", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_scenario_based_ai_dialogue_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat100_supplement_scenario_based_ai_dialogue"], "stem": "Why label each turn (SET CONTEXT, NARROW, STRESS-TEST) instead of writing one long prompt?", "options": ["Labels make the prompt look more professional.", "Labels let a reader see which turn caused which change in the output.", "Labels force the AI to answer faster.", "Labels are required by every AI tool."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Labeled turns make the cause of each change traceable; one giant prompt hides which instruction did the work.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_one_giant_prompt", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_4_2_one_giant_prompt", "pattern": "everything_in_one_turn", "description": "Cramming scenario, constraints, and stress tests into a single huge prompt instead of separate labeled turns", "trigger": "learner writes one long prompt and reads only the final answer", "feedback": "Split the dialogue into l